Q: What&#39;s the simplest way to reverse the order of bits in a byte in C/C++? A: <p>On modern architecture, bit twiddling hacks are pessimal. They get in the way of the compiler figuring out what you’re trying to do and generating efficient <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Single_instruction,_multiple_data" rel="nofollow noreferrer">SIMD</a> code for it.</p>
<p>The following <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/C%2B%2B20" rel="nofollow noreferrer">C++20</a> implementation, compiled literally without optimizations, would copy each byte of input to a bitfield, copy the bits to a second bitfield one by one, then copy that intermediate result to the output. (Before C++20, in order to avoid <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Undefined_behavior" rel="nofollow noreferrer">undefined behavior</a>, it would be necessary to call <code>memcpy()</code> rather than <code>std::bit_Cast</code>, which would prevent the function from being <code>constexpr</code>.) In reality, since that’s straightforward enough for the compiler to figure out what you’re doing, many mainstream compilers can optimize it.</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;bit&gt;
#include &lt;cstdint&gt;

using std::uint8_t;

struct Bitfield8 {
    uint8_t b0 : 1;
    uint8_t b1 : 1;
    uint8_t b2 : 1;
    uint8_t b3 : 1;
    uint8_t b4 : 1;
    uint8_t b5 : 1;
    uint8_t b6 : 1;
    uint8_t b7 : 1;
};

constexpr uint8_t revByte(const uint8_t in) noexcept
{
    const Bitfield8 inBits = std::bit_cast&lt;Bitfield8&gt;(in);
    const Bitfield8 outBits = {
        .b0 = inBits.b7,
        .b1 = inBits.b6,
        .b2 = inBits.b5,
        .b3 = inBits.b4,
        .b4 = inBits.b3,
        .b5 = inBits.b2,
        .b6 = inBits.b1,
        .b7 = inBits.b0
    };
    return std::bit_cast&lt;uint8_t&gt;(outBits);
}
</code></pre>
<p>With a bare-bones test driver:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;algorithm&gt; // transform
#include &lt;cstdlib&gt;
#include &lt;iterator&gt;  // begin, cbegin, cend

alignas(16) extern uint8_t data[1024];

int main()
{
    std::transform(std::cbegin(data), std::cend(data), std::begin(data), revByte);
    return EXIT_SUCCESS;
}
</code></pre>
<p>Clang 21.1.0 with <a href="https://clang.llvm.org/docs/ClangCommandLineReference.html#optimization-level" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>-O3</code></a> <a href="https://clang.llvm.org/docs/ClangCommandLineReference.html#cmdoption-clang-march" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>-march=armv9.6-a</code></a> <a href="https://clang.llvm.org/docs/ClangCommandLineReference.html#cmdoption-clang-funroll-loops" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>-fno-unroll-loops</code></a> will recognize what’s going on here and optimize to a single <code>rbit</code> vector instruction:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>.LBB0_1:
        ldr     q0, [x9, x8]
        rbit    v0.16b, v0.16b
        str     q0, [x9, x8]
        add     x8, x8, #16
        cbne    x8, x10, .LBB0_1
</code></pre>
<p>And without <code>-fno-unroll-loops</code>, it unrolls and inlines the entire loop into a series of interleaved  <code>ldp</code>, <code>rbit</code>, <code>stp</code> instructions.</p>
<p><a href="https://www.intel.com/content/www/us/en/developer/articles/guide/porting-guide-for-icc-users-to-dpcpp-or-icx.html#inpage-nav-undefined" rel="nofollow noreferrer">ICX</a> 2026 or Clang 22.1 with <code>-O3 -march=x86-64-v4 -fno-unroll-loops</code> auto-vectorizes this to:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>.LBB0_1:
        vmovdqu ymm3, ymmword ptr [rcx + rax]
        vpand   ymm4, ymm3, ymm0
        vpshufb ymm4, ymm1, ymm4
        vpsrlw  ymm3, ymm3, 4
        vpand   ymm3, ymm3, ymm0
        vpshufb ymm3, ymm2, ymm3
        vpor    ymm3, ymm4, ymm3
        vmovdqu ymmword ptr [rcx + rax], ymm3
        add     rax, 32
        cmp     rax, 1024
        jne     .LBB0_1
</code></pre>
<p>Here, <code>ymm0</code> is a nibble-mask <code>0x0F</code>, and <code>ymm1</code> and <code>ymm2</code> have been pre-initialized to lookup tables for the high and low nibbles before the loop.</p>
<p>Without <code>-fno-unroll-loops</code>, Clang loads four 128-bit vectors from memory on each iteration and ICX loads two. <a href="https://gcc.gnu.org/gcc-16/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">GCC 16.1</a> (2026-04-30) generates 32-bit shift and bitwise instructions, still roughly as good as the versions micro-optimized for 32-bit operations but missing the <code>rbit</code> optimization on ARM64. <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Microsoft_Visual_C%2B%2B" rel="nofollow noreferrer">MSVC</a> 19.50 for x64 doesn’t handle this well at all, using 8-bit operations, and MSVC for ARM64 does not even inline <code>revByte</code>, but generates <code>bl</code> instructions to it within the inner loop.</p>
<p>This is a good example of how, on good compilers, readable high-level code can optimize better than hacks designed for 20th-century hardware, but if the optimizer gets confused, it can be much worse than even a naive loop. You don’t want to get the compiler to generate 32-bit code for a target with 128-bit registers!</p>

Q: Fonts for different languages? A: <p>As explained by <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/79962932/15015675">Alohci</a> the main problem with your current CSS is the font file reference:</p>
<p><strong>Google font API URLs are dynamic CSS queries not font file URLs</strong> – returning parameter-based CSS rules (also including agent detection a.k.a »browser-sniffing«).</p>
<p>You can easily check it by entering the URL in your <a href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Arimo:ital,wght@0,400..700;1,400..700&amp;display=swap" rel="nofollow noreferrer">address bar</a></p>
<p>The <code>src</code> property <code>@font-face</code> expects a URL to a specific font-file – <code>woff2</code>,  <code>woff</code>, <code>ttf</code> or <code>otf</code>.</p>
<h2>Browser font loading is property agnostic</h2>
<p>By »property« I'm referring to intrinsic font properties such as the font family name, font-weight, style (italic or upright) etc. as specified in the font files data records. In other words:</p>
<p>The browser doesn't care, whether the referenced »Arimo« font-file is actually a proper »Arimo« or e.g. a »Alef« or if it is <em>intrinsically</em> bold, regular or italic.</p>
<p>That said – you can easily map arbitrary font files to any »font-family« context as you like.</p>
<h3>»Language« based font switching?</h3>
<p>As you already noticed, the crucial CSS property is <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Reference/At-rules/@font-face/unicode-range" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>unicode-range</code></a> mapping files according to certain unicode ranges (similarly to weight/style based mapping).</p>
<pre class="lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>/* Latin */
unicode-range: U+0000-00FF, U+0131, U+0152-0153, U+02BB-02BC, U+02C6, U+02DA, U+02DC, U+0304, U+0308, U+0329, U+2000-206F, U+20AC, U+2122, U+2191, U+2193, U+2212, U+2215, U+FEFF, U+FFFD;

/* Hebrew*/
unicode-range: U+0307-0308, U+0590-05FF, U+200C-2010, U+20AA, U+25CC, U+FB1D-FB4F;
</code></pre>
<p>To be precise, the browser is actually not detecting any language but swaps between fonts according to the unicode-ranges as required per string inputs/markup.</p>
<p>This is what the google fonts API also heavily utilizes:<br />
Font-files are split into subsets: e.g latin, latin-extended, vietnamese, hebrew etc.</p>
<h3>Font-family mapping</h3>
<p>Here's a simplified  example »merging« font-families Arimo and Alef for Latin and Hebrew unicode ranges:</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>/* latin – map to Alef (only static) */
@font-face {
  font-family: 'AlefArimo';
  font-style: normal;
  font-weight: 400;
  src: url(https://fonts.gstatic.com/s/alef/v24/FeVfS0NQpLYgnjVRCg.woff2) format('woff2');
  unicode-range: U+0000-00FF, U+0131, U+0152-0153, U+02BB-02BC, U+02C6, U+02DA, U+02DC, U+0304, U+0308, U+0329, U+2000-206F, U+20AC, U+2122, U+2191, U+2193, U+2212, U+2215, U+FEFF, U+FFFD;
}

/* hebrew – map to Arimo */
@font-face {
  font-family: 'AlefArimo';
  font-style: normal;
  font-weight: 400;
  src: url(https://fonts.gstatic.com/s/arimo/v36/P5sfzZCDf9_T_3cV7NCUECyoxNk37cxcAhrBZQI.woff2) format('woff2');
  unicode-range: U+0307-0308, U+0590-05FF, U+200C-2010, U+20AA, U+25CC, U+FB1D-FB4F;
}

h3 {
  font-size: 1rem;
  margin: 0;
}

p {
  margin: 0;
}

body {
  font-size: 5em;
}

.arimo-alef {
  font-family: 'AlefArimo';
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;h3&gt;Static fonts&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p class="arimo-alef"&gt;
  &lt;span&gt;Hello World&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span&gt;
  שלום עולם
  &lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<p>Basically, we're just »Frankensteining« – so mixing <code>@font-face</code> rules coming from different query results to a get a customized font-loading context. In the above example I renamed the new font-family property reference to <code>AlefArimo</code> for the sake of illustration – you could use any name you want.</p>
<h3>Language based font loading overhead?</h3>
<p>Let's say you page is mostly English – for instance for an event site.</p>
<p>There is a good chance the browser rarely has to load e.g the font-file required for rendering latin-extended or the Vietnamese subset ranges.</p>
<p>But there is also a great chance a »non-latin« string will pop up – for instance a name from a Polish, Hungarian or Vietnamese director/artist.<br />
The browser needs to load the required subsets – if available (or use a local fallback).</p>
<p>However, all modern browsers are actually pretty smart (or »lazy«) detecting the required  font file assets.<br />
So you may ditch provided certain ranges for the sake of loading times (as you're reducing the overall CSS that has to be loaded and parsed) but leaving unused subset rules will most likely not <em>significantly</em> impact your rending times.</p>
<h3>2026: Browser inconsistencies?</h3>
<p>In your case you want to mix »Arimo« with »Alef« where the latter is only available as a <a href="https://fonts.google.com/specimen/Alef" rel="nofollow noreferrer">static font</a>.</p>
<p>Unfortunately, Chromium/Blink based browsers seem to struggle with a mix of static and variable fonts mapped to a font-family – while it works flawlessly in Firefox.</p>
<p><a href="https://i.sstatic.net/nurt7wvP.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/nurt7wvP.png" alt="Firefox" /></a><br />
<em>Firefox/Gecko</em></p>
<p><a href="https://i.sstatic.net/r39g9qkZ.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/r39g9qkZ.png" alt="Chromium" /></a><br />
<em>Chromium/Blink: Latin is ignored</em></p>
